+# Runtime and dependency review
+
+Reviewed 2026-10-03 from `d70014342f1995807bccd26370a7b86379c89099` without changing
+application code, package manifests or locks. This dashboard is not ready for
+public hosting with its current dependency tree.
+
+Linux Node 24.21.0 passes the 173 contract tests, 13 socket tests and the actual
+efsn WSS/PostgreSQL/HTTPS/compiled-browser scenario. The latter uses PostgreSQL
+16.15 and Ubuntu nginx `1.24.0-2ubuntu7.18`. Four browser checkpoints include
+writer loss, expiry and recovery. These results establish a runtime candidate,
+not a fresh-install or public deployment acceptance. The existing frontend build
+was used for that browser run. The chain was stationary.
+
+The separate `CI=true` Node 24 production build also succeeds in new scratch
+space. Its 547 files match the earlier build except for the JavaScript source
+map. The dependency warning `DEP0176` (`fs.F_OK`) is retained in the evidence.
+No dependency installation, legacy crypto option or lint bypass was used.
+
+Node 24 is [LTS](https://nodejs.org/en/about/previous-releases); select a reviewed
+current patch and npm version in the eventual release manifest. The older
+Node 22.11.0 remains only a recorded test baseline. PostgreSQL 16 is still a
+[supported major](https://www.postgresql.org/support/versioning/). Keep Ubuntu's
+nginx package revision and security backports in the review rather than judging
+only its upstream version; [USN-8563-5](https://ubuntu.com/security/notices/USN-8563-5)
+covers one correction present in the tested revision. Host libraries and future
+updates still need review at release.
+
+Read-only npm 10.9.0 lock audits returned 44 root/backend findings (12 critical,
+21 high, six moderate, five low), 76 frontend findings (69 high, four moderate,
+three low), and seven separate legacy API findings (four high, three low).
+These include dependency-level findings and are not unique CVE or reachable
+exploit counts. Build tools live in `dependencies`, so omitting devDependencies
+would not remove them. No automatic audit fix was used.
+
+Required follow-up, in order:
+
+1. Review and update Primus 6.1.0 and its resolved ws 1.1.5 together. The latter
+   falls within [GHSA-96hv-2xvq-fx4p](https://github.com/websockets/ws/security/advisories/GHSA-96hv-2xvq-fx4p).
+   Application message validation does not establish a fix for parser memory
+   accounting. The old Primus transformer uses `socket.upgradeReq`, so merely
+   replacing ws is not an established compatible change. Verify both plugins,
+   native efsn framing, authentication, limits and reconnects with ordinary tests.
+2. Review Lodash 3.10.1, Express 4.17.1 and geoip-lite 1.2.1, removing unused
+   legacy tools rather than blindly updating every installed package. Lodash's
+   old `pluck`/`sortByOrder` calls need explicit compatibility work. GeoIP's audit
+   finding is inherited through `unzip`; distinguish lookup from data installation.
+3. Consolidate API dependency installation. The tested API resolves root Express;
+   there is no local `api-server/node_modules`. Its separate legacy lock must not
+   silently override the tested root version in a deployment.
+4. Update or replace browser Axios 0.21.2 and review unused/build-only packages.
+   Axios is the only flagged package found among 41 package names represented
+   in the current compiled JavaScript maps; it uses XHR. This does not clear all
+   other assets or all Axios findings. Preserve freshness, cancellation and
+   timeout behavior. Review the [deprecated CRA build](https://react.dev/blog/2025/02/14/sunsetting-create-react-app)
+   separately from transport changes.
+5. Replace the inherited workflow before enabling deployment. It currently uses
+   Node 16, `npm install` and the foundation's S3 target. No workflow was run and
+   no public target was provisioned during this review.
+
+Repeat the audit and integration checks after these changes and a clean install
+with the final pinned Node/npm versions. Record dispositions for unresolved
+findings. Finish service supervision, certificates/renewal, retries/log retention
+and the coordinated deployment profile before public use.
+
+The accompanying efsn investigation retains the complete report in
+`docs/restart-dashboard-runtime.md` and exact locks, registry responses, source
+hashes and local test evidence under
+`docs/evidence/restart-dashboard-runtime-review-2026-10-03/`.
